:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+2p(1)q(1)+\\frac{1}{10}p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(3+0)(0)+(3+1)(3-k)+\\frac{1}{10}(3+2)(6-4k) \\\\  & =0+(12-4k)+(3-2k) \\\\  & =15-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 15-6k & =0 \\\\ 6k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1-2t$ and $q(t)=2-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fc18bd8-0e1d-5f2f-b412-9e10113b7a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360795 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=2+kt$ and $q(t)=t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee3f973-bbbf-5982-80f9-a2004f5870df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360796 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(2-k)(-1-1)+(2+k)(1-1)+(2+2k)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-4+2k)+0+(-4-4k) \\\\  & =-8-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -8-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(4)y(4)+x(-4)y(-4)+x(-5)y(-5).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k+t$ and $q(t)=1+t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10a41eac-4d39-5c59-85ee-b6fa71b11cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360797 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(4)q(4)+p(-4)q(-4)+p(-5)q(-5) \\\\  & =(k+4)(1+4-16)+(k-4)(1-4-16)+(k-5)(1-5-25) \\\\  & =(-11k-44)+(-19k+76)+(-29k+145) \\\\  & =-59k+177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -59k+177 & =0 \\\\ 59k & =177 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81d22276-2b6e-5fad-b0ea-dfad72ebe3fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360810 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453051 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453052 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453053 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c591f6d1-f8d9-5efb-9959-52acee26e120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360814 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453057 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453058 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453059 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360816 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements 